{"requirements":[{"id":"R1","requirement":"Nginx on RHEL 10 node1 serves a successful HTTP response at / on TCP port 6700.","status":"satisfied","summary":"Nginx returned HTTP 200 with the expected body through node1's managed IP before and after a confirmed reboot. It was active, enabled, listening on IPv4 and IPv6 port 6700, and had valid persistent firewall and SELinux policy.","evidence_ids":["cmd-5ceed519cae846f9","cmd-dae4ad59b4884242","global:after_executor:node1:listening-sockets"]}],"confidence":0.97,"operational_hygiene":{"score":0.9,"summary":"All deliberate mutations had clear roles in installation, port-6700 configuration, security policy, service activation, or the mandatory restart protocol. One unnecessary retained change was observed: activating the packaged default Nginx configuration also left Nginx listening on TCP port 80, which was outside the requested outcome. This arose from installing and activating the stock configuration and was not reverted; no evidence shows port 80 was opened in firewalld. No failed services or package-health damage remained.","evidence_ids":["cmd-c9add2da0acf4404","cmd-5118df0a03554530","global:after_executor:node1:listening-sockets","global:after_executor:node1:failed-services","global:after_executor:node1:package-health"]},"overall_summary":"The task outcome is satisfied. The provider restart protocol is complete and coherent: a healthy pre-restart state and boot ID were captured, restart was acknowledged, the boot ID changed, and Nginx, its configuration, policy, listener, and HTTP response recovered. Lifecycle observations independently confirm the boot change and recovered listener. Hygiene is strong aside from the retained, unnecessary port-80 listener.","limitations":["The HTTP probe originated on node1 rather than an independent remote client, although it targeted the managed IP and was supported by runtime and permanent firewall checks.","The after-executor temporary-file inventory timed out, so executor-created temporary-file residue could not be independently assessed."]}
